  "author": "whirligig",
  "version": "1",
  "notes": "A conventional 1.2 m high-wing trainer, the reference article for Phase 1. Nothing exotic: a cambered Clark-Y-ish wing with dihedral and washout, a symmetric tailplane with a generous elevator, and a fin. It exists to prove that ordinary aeroplane behaviour falls out of the element model before anything strange is attempted — it should trim where a trainer trims, glide at L/D 8-12, and drop its nose at the stall without being told to. A 1000 kV motor on three cells swings a 10x4 propeller on the nose; its torque reaction rolls the aircraft left, as a single-engine model really does. The fuselage itself is a single `drag` point of 0.0035 m^2 equivalent flat-plate area standing in for the body, the struts and two exposed wheels until `body` lofts arrive in Phase 6. Datum is the wing quarter chord: +x forward, +y right, +z down. The CG lands about 29% of chord, roughly 9 mm aft of the datum, giving a static margin in the high teens.",
